// Reference answer for GUML-Bench task `v01-event-filters`.
//
// # This answer used to fail a checklist item on purpose. It no longer does.
//
// The task requires three filters composed **client-side**: "one fetch, not one per change". `where=` takes
// a single enumerated state, and until `of=` existed a repeater's source had to be a declared *resource* — so
// a predicate over three states could be computed in a `js` block and then had nothing to feed. This file
// filtered on the server and failed "filtering does not refetch", with a note saying so.
//
// `list matches of=Event` closes it: the source is the `js`-computed array and `of=` names the row type, so
// `{name}` and `{country}` resolve against `Event`'s fields exactly as a resource's rows would. One fetch,
// three composed filters, and the counts and the list read the same array.
//
// One escape hatch, for the one thing with no binding form: a conjunction over three states. Everything
// else — the fetch, the loading state, the error state, the empty state, the segmented control, the reset —
// is vocabulary.

page Events title="Events" description="Filter the event stream and see what is left." lang=en

// `hits`, not `count`. A field named `count` collides with the `.count` aggregate — `events.count` is
// unambiguously the row count, so `{events.count.sum}` lowered to `events.length.reduce(…)`, which is not
// valid TypeScript and was caught by `typecheck-emitted`. Renaming the field is the honest fix; a language
// where a field can shadow an aggregate keyword would need a disambiguating syntax nobody wants.
type Event {id, name, channel, country, hits:number}

data events:Event[] GET /api/events

state channel=all|web|ios|android
state region=all|GB|US|DE|IN
state busy=false

js
  // The one construct with no binding form: three filters composed. `where=` takes a single enumerated
  // state, so the conjunction lives here — and the `list` below iterates the result, which is what `of=`
  // made possible. `region` rather than `country` because a row field shadows a state of the same name.
  const matches = events.filter(
    (e) =>
      (channel === "all" || e.channel === channel) &&
      (region === "all" || e.country === region) &&
      (!busy || e.hits > 100),
  );
  const total = matches.reduce((a, e) => a + e.hits, 0);

section
  h1 Events

  toolbar
    tabs channel
    select region aria="Country"
    check {busy} aria="Only events above 100" >busy = !busy
    btn Reset outline >channel = all; region = all; busy = false

  grid cols=2
    stat "Matching events" {matches.count}
    stat "Total count" {total}

  list matches of=Event
    text {name}
    note {country}
    text {hits}
    empty Nothing matches these filters.
